  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "William has a desk with two drawers, $A$ and $B.$ In drawer $A$ he has $14$ markers, of which $3$ are blue. In drawer $B$ he has $21$ markers, of which $4$ are blue. What is the probability that a randomly selected marker from either drawer is blue?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $U$ be the event that the chosen marker is blue, and let $A$ be the event that the marker was selected from drawer $A.$ Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the marker was selected from drawer $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marker is blue, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(U)=P(U\\cap A)+P(U\\cap {A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(U\\cap A),$ we take the number of blue markers in drawer $A$ $(3)$ and divide by the total number of markers in both drawers $(14+21=35).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(U\\cap A)=\\frac{3}{35}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(U\\cap {A}^{\u2032}),$ we take the number of blue markers on drawer $B$ $(4)$ and divide by the total number of markers in both drawers $(35).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(U\\cap {A}^{\u2032})=\\frac{4}{35}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marker from the desk drawers is blue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U) & =P(U\\cap A)+P(U\\cap {A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{35}+\\frac{4}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
